  "technical_issues": [
    {
      "where": "2.5 Mission Duration & EOL",
      "issue": "The stated orbital-decay values are approximately ten times too large for the given equation and inputs. At 600 km, Δa per orbit is about 0.040 m for 0.3 m² and 0.57 m for 4.3 m², not 0.40 m and 5.8 m. Corresponding initial decay rates are about 0.22 km/year and 3.1 km/year; the H/rate estimates are about 320 years and 23 years, respectively, not 32 and 2.2 years. Thus the report does not establish that a 4 m² sail meets the 25-year guideline."
    },
    {
      "where": "2.5 Mission Duration & EOL",
      "issue": "The claim of about 11 km altitude loss over five years follows the same factor-of-ten error; the stated inputs imply about 1.1 km using the report's approximation."
    },
    {
      "where": "2.3 Orbit",
      "issue": "The five-day access estimate divides equatorial ground-track spacing by the maximum roll-reachable width. It is a simplified upper-bound argument and does not demonstrate actual reef-specific revisit or imaging opportunities, which depend on ground-track geometry, pointing, lighting, and cloud conditions."
    },
    {
      "where": "2.4 Ground Control & Communications",
      "issue": "A 50 Mbit/s downlink is assumed without a link budget. The report acknowledges this gap, so the stated capacity margin is not yet verified against transmitter power, antenna gains, range, coding, and losses."
    }
  ],
  "overall": "A clear, well-supported preliminary mission design with strong mission rationale, useful STK evidence, and detailed orbit and communications work. The orbital-decay arithmetic error materially invalidates the claimed drag-sail lifetime and EOL compliance; the link budget and revisit analysis also need validation. Recalculate disposal performance and verify communications and coverage before treating the design as feasible."
}
